From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 08:44:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fonts: Only load @font-face fonts that the page uses

Every @font-face rule was fetched, sanitised and kept in memory as soon as
its stylesheet applied, even when no style used its family or its
unicode-range covered no character on the page. CSS Fonts 4 requires user
agents to only download fonts that are used.

A new rule now only registers an unloaded face, matched by its descriptors.
Font matching asks for the face once it selects it for a character, and the
loads it asked for start after layout. `FontFace.load()` and
`FontFaceSet.load()` ask for faces the same way. Faces whose sources are all
`local()` are still loaded right away.

Because matching now decides which faces load, adding @font-face rules
dirties the document so that text is matched again, `document.fonts.ready`
flushes layout, and a reflow that starts loads makes it pending again.

Testing: A new WPT test checks that unused faces stay "unloaded". Two canvas
tests that expected fonts not used by the page to be loaded now fail, as they
do in Chrome, Firefox and Safari.

Fork note: second of four patches; meant for upstream, same code as the
upstream-based version except the promise API. Motivation: generated-UI pages
that link Google Fonts CJK families declare hundreds of unicode-range subsets
(441 @font-face rules on the "keai" concert-article test page), and Servo
downloaded all of them (437 files, 10.5 MiB) where ArkWeb downloads 64.

Relies on: the previous patch (the load state on `FontFaceRuleInfo`, which is
how matching tells unloaded faces apart and how `FontFace.status` shows it);
the FontFaceSet.load backport (its font query is what makes
`document.fonts.load()` ask for faces); matching going through
`FontGroupFamilyTemplate::font_if_matches` and `char_in_unicode_range`
(components/shared/fonts/font_template.rs); `LayoutThread::reflow` returning a
`ReflowResult` and `can_skip_reflow_request_entirely` gating it; and the
readiness checks in `Document::maybe_fulfill_font_ready_promise` and
`Window::maybe_resolve_pending_screenshot_readiness_requests` reading
`FontContext::web_fonts_still_loading`.

Drop when upstream loads @font-face rules lazily: the test this patch adds,
css/css-font-loading/fontface-unused-stays-unloaded.html, passes on plain
upstream.

Validation: `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 1 css/css-font-loading css/css-fonts html/canvas/element/text html/canvas/offscreen/text` (948 tests): the new fontface-unused-stays-unloaded passes; 2d.text.draw.fontface.notinpage and six "(drawing text in a canvas)" subtests of generic-family-keywords-003 now fail as in Chrome/Firefox/Safari; matching/font-unicode-PUA subtests are intermittent (test race, both results seen); the only other unexpected result, font-variant-emoji-1 FAIL, also happens on plain ohos-main. Host release build, keai page served locally over HTTP/1.1 keep-alive (3 runs each): font files fetched 437 -> 66, RSS 525.3 -> 471.6 MiB, heap 340.1 -> 286.4 MiB, `--exit -o` screenshot byte-identical to ohos-main. The servo-webfont-eager-download reproducer (6 @font-face rules, 1 needed) fetches 1 font instead of 6. On a PLR-AL00, production build with `--features tracing-hitrace`, the keai page served from the host through `hdc rport`, measured with this patch and the next two applied (they change invalidation, not which fonts load): 437 -> 66 font files per load in each of 5 `servoperf page` loads, and PSS 30 s after launch 507.6 -> 467.9 MB (-7.8%, 4 interleaved loads each, every pair 35-49 MB apart).
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; designed with the maintainer, written and tested by the agent, ported from upstream main to ohos-main's Rc<Promise> API)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
